---
import Marketing from "../layouts/Marketing.astro";
import pelican from "../assets/screenshots/session-pelican.png";
import builder from "../assets/screenshots/automation-builder.png";
import compose from "../assets/screenshots/automation-compose.png";
import operator from "../assets/screenshots/operator-overview.png";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const docs = `${base}/docs`;

const logos = Object.entries(
  import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>("../assets/connector-logos/*.svg", { eager: true }),
)
  .map(([path, mod]) => {
    const slug = path.split("/").pop()!.replace(".svg", "");
    return { slug, src: mod.default.src, label: slug.replace(/_/g, " ") };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => a.slug.localeCompare(b.slug));

const numbers = [
  { figure: "< 1 s", caption: "cold start from an image's base snapshot, with no pre-warmed pool" },
  { figure: "< 100 ms", caption: "resume on the host that took the snapshot" },
  { figure: "1–2 s", caption: "resume on any other host, which is also the cost of migrating a session" },
  { figure: "≈ 100 GiB", caption: "blob storage for a thousand sessions of a 4 GiB image, not 4 TiB" },
];
---

<Marketing
  title="engrams"
  description="A self-hosted orchestrator for AI coding agents. Runs are started by schedules, Slack threads, pull requests, and webhooks; each one gets its own microVM on servers you own and keeps its record."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Where your coding agents do the routine work.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        engrams runs Claude Code and Codex in Firecracker microVMs on servers you own. A schedule,
        a Slack thread, a pull request, or a webhook starts a run. Every run keeps its transcript,
        its diff, and its snapshot, and a person is one message away.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${docs}/getting-started/local-quickstart/`}>Run it locally</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href={`${docs}/guides/deploy-overview/`}>Deploy to your cloud</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="https://github.com/cortexapps/engrams">Source on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shot">
    <div class="container">
      <img src={pelican.src} width={pelican.width} height={pelican.height} alt="A session in the engrams dashboard: the agent has drawn a pelican riding a bicycle and shared the PNG in the transcript; the right pane shows the session's profile, its changed files, and three published apps." loading="eager" />
      <p class="caption">A session, asked for a PNG of a pelican on a bicycle, with no image libraries and no internet. It wrote a PNG encoder in Python and shared the file in the thread.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>1. Enable an image</h3>
          <p>
            Push any Linux image with <code>/bin/sh</code> to a registry and enable it once.
            engrams writes it as content-addressed chunks, boots it on the fleet, runs your
            warm-up command, and freezes a base snapshot.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>2. Start a run</h3>
          <p>
            A person types a prompt, or a trigger fires. The host restores the snapshot into a
            fresh microVM, mounts the harness, and the agent starts with its caches already warm.
            The transcript and every tool call stream to the dashboard, to Slack, or to the CLI.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>3. Snapshot, resume, fork</h3>
          <p>
            When the agent goes idle the VM is snapshotted to chunks and destroyed. The next
            prompt restores it, on any host. Forking a session is a manifest copy of a few
            kilobytes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-pane">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Automations</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The routine work is the point. An automation is one trigger, a tree of blocks, and typed
        inputs: start a session, send it a prompt, wait for it, run a command inside it, post to
        Slack or GitHub, branch, loop. Runs are durable, so a run survives a restart and can wait
        hours for a reply without holding anything open.
      </p>
      <div class="grid two">
        <figure class="shot">
          <img src={compose.src} width={compose.width} height={compose.height} alt="The New automation page asks what the automation should do; a drafting agent assembles it on the canvas." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Describe it in a sentence and a drafting agent assembles the automation on the canvas. Nothing runs until you enable it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img src={builder.src} width={builder.width} height={builder.height} alt="The automation builder: a schedule trigger and a create-session block on a canvas, with the trigger's cron and timezone in the inspector." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Or build it by hand. The trigger is the first node; a plus on any edge inserts a block.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="grid three" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>One run per thread</h3>
          <p>
            Mention the bot in Slack and a session opens for that thread, as the person who asked,
            with their credentials. Replies join the same run. The thread relays the agent's
            messages and questions both ways.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Pull request review</h3>
          <p>
            A finder and a verifier review each pull request on the repositories you list, and
            post the confirmed findings as one GitHub review. A new push supersedes the review in
            flight.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The record</h3>
          <p>
            Every run is a step timeline. Each step opens to its inputs, its outputs, its output
            text, and the session it used. Trace spans for every model call and tool call can go
            to Langfuse or any OpenTelemetry collector.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button ghost" href={`${docs}/concepts/automations/`}>How automations work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why engrams</h2>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div>
          <h3>It runs in your cloud</h3>
          <p>
            One Terraform apply and two Helm releases bring it up on GKE or EKS, with GCS or S3
            for blobs and your cloud's secret manager for credentials. No transcript, workspace,
            or key leaves your account.
          </p>
          <h3>Every session is a real virtual machine</h3>
          <p>
            Isolation is KVM, enforced by hardware. Outbound traffic from a session goes through
            a filtering proxy on the host, so an agent reaches only the hosts you allow, and
            credentials are brokered at that proxy rather than handed to the agent.
          </p>
          <h3>Any agent runtime</h3>
          <p>
            Claude Code and Codex ship as harnesses. A harness is a bundle the host mounts at
            boot, so your image never contains the agent, and you can register your own.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Idle sessions cost nothing to keep</h3>
          <p>
            A paused session is a set of chunks in the blob store, deduplicated against its
            image and every other session, and a row in Postgres. It holds no host resources
            until the next prompt. Your own model keys, or OpenRouter, pay for the tokens.
          </p>
          <h3>Storage is deduplicated by construction</h3>
          <p>
            Chunks are named by their hash, so a base image is stored once no matter how many
            sessions use it, and a snapshot writes only what changed.
          </p>
          <h3>An operator's view</h3>
          <p>
            Hosts, capacity, live sandboxes, snapshot durability, and what needs attention, on
            one page. Drain a host and its sessions move.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-pane shot">
    <div class="container">
      <img src={operator.src} width={operator.width} height={operator.height} alt="The Operator page: capacity and locality gauges, fleet counts, and storage durability figures." loading="lazy" />
      <p class="caption">The Operator page for a two-host fleet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="numbers">
        {numbers.map((n) => (
          <div>
            <div class="figure">{n.figure}</div>
            <div class="caption">{n.caption}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        Measured on the reference deployment: GKE, C3 nodes, Firecracker with lazy memory paging.
        Measure your own fleet before you promise them to anyone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-pane">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What engrams is not</h2>
      <ul class="nots">
        <li>
          <strong>Not a hosted service.</strong> You run it. If you want someone else to run the
          sandboxes, E2B and Modal do that well.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a development environment for people.</strong> Sessions exist for agents.
          You can open a shell or an IDE into one, but workspaces for engineers at their editors
          are what Coder is for.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a container runner.</strong> Production isolation is Firecracker on Linux
          with KVM. The macOS backend and the process backend exist for development only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a model provider.</strong> You bring an Anthropic or OpenAI key, or point a
          harness at OpenRouter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not finished.</strong> A 0.x project that runs one company's production. Wire
          formats and APIs change, and it carries a fork of Firecracker that is rebased on
          upstream daily.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Agents, clouds, and integrations</h2>
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Harnesses</h3>
          <p>
            Claude Code and Codex, with their models, a Build and a Plan mode, and effort levels.
            Either can be pointed at OpenRouter. Or bring your own.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Backends</h3>
          <p>
            Firecracker on Linux with KVM for production. Apple Virtualization on a Mac and plain
            subprocesses for development.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Clouds</h3>
          <p>
            GKE and EKS with Terraform quickstarts and Helm charts. GCS or S3 for chunks, Secret
            Manager or Secrets Manager with KMS for credentials.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Built-in connectors</h3>
      <p class="muted">
        Credentials for these are held by engrams and brokered at the egress proxy, so an agent
        can call the API without ever holding the key.
      </p>
      <div class="logo-wall">
        {logos.map((l) => (
          <figure>
            <img src={l.src} alt="" width="32" height="32" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>{l.label}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-pane">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Start</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The local quickstart runs the whole stack on one machine in a few minutes. The
        deployment guides take a fresh GCP project or AWS account to a running fleet.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${docs}/getting-started/local-quickstart/`}>Run it locally</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href={`${docs}/guides/deploy-gcp/`}>Deploy on GCP</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href={`${docs}/guides/deploy-aws/`}>Deploy on AWS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Marketing>
